    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">hey all<div><br></div><div>a reasonably straightforward  use of <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">plyr</font>, when you have a data set where durations&#39; start and conclusion are encoded in variables. consider:</div><div><br></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">d1 &lt;- data.frame(indiv = 1:2,</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">                 start = c(2002, 2006),</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">                 end = c(2008, 2009))</font></div></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">&gt;d1<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">  indiv start  end<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">1     1  2002 2008<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">2     2  2006 2009</font></blockquote><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div>if I wanted  to make each row a person.year, the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">plyr </font>syntax is trival</div><div><br></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">head(ddply(d1, .(indiv), function(i) data.frame(years = seq(i$start, i$end, 1))))</font></div></div><div><br></div><div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\"><span style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\">indiv years<br></span><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">1     1  2002<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">2     1  2003<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">3     1  2004<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">4     1  2005<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">5     1  2006<br></font><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">6     1  2007</font></blockquote></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div>but translating this to <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">dplyr</font> eludes me. both of these fail:</div><div><br></div><div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">tbl_df(d1) %&gt;%</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    group_by(indiv) %&gt;%</font></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">    data.frame(years = seq(start, end, 1))</font></div></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div><div style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\">tbl_df(d1) %&gt;%</div><div style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\">    group_by(indiv) %&gt;%</div><div style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\">    summarize(years = seq(start, end, 1))</div><div style=\"font-family:&#39;courier new&#39;,monospace\"><br></div><div>how can you do this in dplyr?</div></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
